== Post-show careers ==
David Atherton published a cookbook after winning the series.<ref>{{Cite web|title=David Atherton|url=https://www.amazon.com/David-Atherton/e/B09237JZJB?ref=sr_ntt_srch_lnk_2&qid=1631372707&sr=1-2|access-date=2021-09-11|website=www.amazon.com|language=en-us}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Walsh|first=Grace|date=2020-09-25|title=Great British Bake Off contestants: Where are they now?|url=https://www.goodto.com/food/bake-off-contestants-where-are-they-now-105581|access-date=2021-09-11|website=GoodtoKnow|language=en}}</ref> Released in 2020, it is a children's cookbook titled ''My First Cookbook''.  Follow-up versions ''My First Green Cookbook'' and ''Bake, Make and Learn to Cook'' released in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=David Atherton|url=https://www.amazon.com/kindle-dbs/entity/author/B09237JZJB?_encoding=UTF8&node=283155&offset=0&pageSize=12&searchAlias=stripbooks&sort=author-sidecar-rank&page=1&langFilter=default#formatSelectorHeader|access-date=2021-12-18|website=www.amazon.com|language=en-us}}</ref> In May 2021, Atherton released ''Good to Eat'', which focuses on delivering healthy twists to classic recipes.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Hodder cooks up storm with 2019 'Bake Off' winner David Atherton {{!}} The Bookseller|url=https://www.thebookseller.com/news/hodder-cook-storm-2019-bake-winner-david-atherton-1226712|access-date=2021-09-11|website=www.thebookseller.com}}</ref>

Steph Blackwell contributed to ''The Big Book of Amazing Cakes,'' and published her own cookbook, ''The Joy of Baking,'' in 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Chilton|first=Charlotte|date=2020-12-11|title=Where Are Your Favorite 'Great British Bake Off' Contestants Now?|url=https://www.delish.com/food-news/g34916711/great-british-bake-off-contestants-then-and-now/|access-date=2021-09-11|website=Delish|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|url=https://www.hachette.co.uk/titles/steph-blackwell/the-joy-of-baking/9781529416039/|title=The Joy of Baking|date=2020-11-27|isbn=978-1-5294-1603-9|language=en-US |last1=Blackwell |first1=Steph |publisher=Quercus }}</ref>

==Ratings==
The first episode drew an overnight audience of 5.7&nbsp;million, down 400,000 from the previous series, but with a higher audience share in its time slot than the previous launch at 30.6%.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/stayingin/tvfilm/great-british-bake-off-launch-episode-loses-400000-viewers-a4222986.html |title=Great British Bake Off launch episode loses 400,000 viewers|first=Katie |last=Rosseinsky |work=London Evening Standard |date=28 August 2019 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://tv.bt.com/tv/tv-news/great-british-bake-off-series-launch-sheds-400000-viewers-11364388574267 |title=Great British Bake Off series launch sheds 400,000 viewers |date=28 August 2019 |publisher=BT TV }}</ref>  The overnight ratings for the final episode dropped by over half a million compared to the previous series with an average audience of 6.9 million viewers.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.standard.co.uk/stayingin/tvfilm/great-british-bake-off-2019-finale-viewers-ratings-a4274166.html |title=Great British Bake Off finale loses more than half a million viewers|first=Tobi|last= Akingbad |work=Evening Standard |date=30 October 2019 }}</ref>

